---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const articleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "Fire protection in the lab container – fire resistance, hazardous-substance storage and means of escape",
  "description": "Structural, active and organisational fire protection in the lab container: DIN 4102-2 and DIN EN 13501, fire-resistance classes F30/F90 and EI 90, construction-product classes A1/A2, mineral wool vs. PUR/PIR, Type 90 safety cabinets to DIN EN 14470-1, TRGS 510/526, DGUV 213-850, ASR A2.2/A2.3 – with verified standards.",
  "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Thomas Boss", "url": "https://planexus.de/en/team/thomas-boss", "jobTitle": "Partner & Technical Director", "worksFor": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Planexus GmbH" } },
  "datePublished": "2026-06-11",
  "dateModified": "2026-06-11",
  "image": "https://planexus.de/attached_assets/generated_images/laborcontainer-brandschutz-feuerwiderstand.png",
  "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Planexus GmbH", "logo": { "@type": "ImageObject", "url": "https://planexus.de/images/planexus-logo.png" } },
  "mainEntityOfPage": { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": "https://planexus.de/en/magazine/lab-container-fire-protection" }
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://planexus.de/en" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Magazine", "item": "https://planexus.de/en/magazine" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Fire protection in the lab container", "item": "https://planexus.de/en/magazine/lab-container-fire-protection" }
  ]
};

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Which fire-resistance class does a lab container need?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "It depends on the building-control classification. Requirements for structural fire protection in the Model Building Code (Musterbauordnung) and all state building codes are measured by building class. A single, free-standing, single-storey lab container generally falls into a low building class with limited requirements on the fire resistance of load-bearing elements. As soon as modules are stacked or joined into larger assemblies, connect to an existing building or are classified as a special building (laboratory), the requirements rise – then fire-resisting separations of F30 to F90 to DIN 4102-2, or EI 30 to EI 90 to DIN EN 13501-2, are required. What always governs is the fire-safety concept in the building application, agreed with the competent building-control authority. We deliver the container with the matching wall build-up and documented classifications." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "What does F90 mean, and is it the same as EI 90?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "F90 is the national fire-resistance class to DIN 4102-2: the element withstands the standard fire test for at least 90 minutes. European DIN EN 13501-2 breaks the blanket F class into differentiated criteria: R (load-bearing capacity/Résistance), E (integrity/Étanchéité) and I (insulation), each with a minutes rating. A non-load-bearing fire wall is classified there as EI 90, a load-bearing and integrity-providing wall as REI 90. F90 and EI 90 therefore describe the same 90-minute protection objective – the European class is simply more precise because it states which function is maintained for 90 minutes. Both standard systems apply equally in Germany; DIN 4102 remains widely used in construction practice." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Which construction products are decisive for fire protection in the lab container?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "The core of the wall and roof panels. A sandwich-construction container can be fabricated with different insulation cores, and their reaction to fire differs fundamentally. Mineral wool is classified to DIN EN 13501-1 as A1, or in combination with steel facings as A2 – i.e. non-combustible. PIR rigid foam typically achieves B-s1,d0 to B-s2,d0 (limited combustibility), PUR foam sits markedly lower at E to F (normally to easily ignitable). For laboratory applications with elevated fire load or special-building classification we use panels with a non-combustible mineral-wool core (A1/A2). That not only meets the structural fire-protection requirement, but at the highest material level – no significant contribution of the wall build-up to the fire load." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "How must hazardous substances and solvents be stored in the lab container?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "To TRGS 510 (storage of hazardous substances in portable containers, edition since 16 February 2021). Flammable liquids belong in a fire-protected safety cabinet to DIN EN 14470-1. The common Type 90 guarantees that the cabinet interior does not heat by more than 180 kelvin for 90 minutes in a fire – so the contents remain well below auto-ignition temperature and staff have time to escape and the fire service time to intervene. The standard also knows Types 15, 30 and 60 with correspondingly shorter protection times. Compressed-gas cylinders are stored in cabinets to DIN EN 14470-2. In addition, segregation prohibitions, technical ventilation, spill trays and quantity thresholds govern safe storage organisation in the container." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Which fire extinguishers are mandatory in the lab container?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Type, extinguishing agent and number follow ASR A2.2 (measures against fires) and the fire hazard in the laboratory. Extinguishers are assigned to fire classes: A (solid, glowing substances), B (liquid and liquefying substances such as solvents, oils, alcohols), C (gases), D (metal fires, for example alkali metals or magnesium) and F (cooking fats and oils). In the laboratory fire class B is almost always relevant; with reactive metals, D with special metal-fire powder is added. CO₂ extinguishers are common in laboratories with sensitive equipment because they extinguish without residue. The required number of extinguishing units (LE) follows from floor area and fire hazard to ASR A2.2. TRGS 526 and DGUV Information 213-850 further specify fire protection in laboratory operation." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "What requirements apply to escape and rescue routes in the lab container?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "ASR A2.3 (escape and rescue routes, new edition March 2022, last amended November 2024) and Chapter 6.2 of DGUV Information 213-850 govern. In laboratories, rescue routes and exits must be present in sufficient number, depending on the substances and working methods used. Where the hazard is elevated, a second, independent escape route is required – in the container therefore a second door or an emergency exit, not only the main entrance. In addition come clear minimum widths of circulation and escape routes, doors that open in the direction of escape, emergency lighting and illuminated rescue signs to ASR A1.3 (DIN EN ISO 7010). We plan door openings, emergency exits and safety signage to suit laboratory size and occupancy directly into the layout." } },
    { "@type": "Question", "name": "Does a lab container need a fire detection and alarm system?", "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Not every container, but many applications benefit from it or require it. An automatic fire detection and alarm system to DIN 14675 in conjunction with DIN VDE 0833-1/-2 is required when the fire-safety concept or the building permit prescribes it – for example at higher building classes, special buildings, unattended night operation with sample storage, or high fire load from solvents and lithium-ion batteries. For many standard lab containers, standards-compliant smoke alarms combined with the necessary extinguishers and a clear alarm arrangement suffice. Where a full FDAS with connection to the fire service is required, we plan and integrate it including the necessary fire-service peripherals. Which level is needed is clarified in the fire-safety concept with the expert and building control." } }
  ]
};
import RelatedArticles from '../../../components/RelatedArticles.astro';
